---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import products from "../../data/products.json";
import siteConfig from "../../data/site.config.json";
import { amazonProductLink, money } from "../../lib/amazon";
import { breadcrumbListJsonLd, faqJsonLd } from "../../lib/schema";

const siteUrl = `https://${siteConfig.domain}`;
const title = `Boilies explained UK ${new Date().getFullYear()} - shelf-life vs freezer, fishmeal vs birdfood`;
const description = `The plain-English guide to UK boilie selection. What the categories actually mean, which type suits your venue and when to switch bait on pressured water.`;

const baitPicks = products.filter((p) => p.category === "boilies-bait");

const faqs = [
  { q: "Are shelf-life boilies really inferior to freezer baits?", a: "Not always. Modern shelf-life baits from top UK brands use quality fishmeals and effective preservatives; the trade-off is slightly lower attractor levels than freezer equivalents. On a busy day-ticket water it rarely matters. On a low-stock pressured venue where every angler is on the same shelf-life boilie, switching to freezer bait genuinely helps." },
  { q: "How long does a bag of boilies actually last on a session?", a: "Depends on your feeding style. Little-and-often anglers get 25-30 fishing hours from 1 kg. Heavy prebaiters put 2-3 kg through a single 48-hour session. As a rough baseline, budget 1 kg per rod per 24 hours of fishing on a moderately-stocked water." },
  { q: "Do carp really tell the difference between boilie brands?", a: "Yes - on pressured waters where fish have seen the same bait for months, changing to a different attractor profile (fishmeal to birdfood, or vice versa) often triggers takes. On new venues or high-stock waters, differences between quality brands are marginal." },
  { q: "Can I use pop-ups from one brand over a bed of freebies from another?", a: "Yes and it often works. A visually contrasting pop-up over a matched-colour freebie bed is a classic combination. What matters is consistent attractor profile - do not mix a heavy fishmeal freebie bed with a fruity birdfood pop-up unless you specifically want the contrast." },
];

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbListJsonLd([
    { label: "Home", url: `${siteUrl}/` },
    { label: "Guides", url: `${siteUrl}/guides/` },
    { label: "Boilies explained", url: `${siteUrl}/guides/boilies-explained/` },
  ]),
  faqJsonLd(faqs),
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  indexable={true}
  crumbs={[
    { label: "Guides" },
    { label: "Boilies explained" },
  ]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="guide-content">
      <div class="page-head">
        <div class="kicker">Bait guide · UK · {new Date().getFullYear()}</div>
        <h1>Boilies explained</h1>
        <p class="lede">The categories every UK bait shelf uses, translated into plain English. What matters for your venue and what to ignore in the marketing.</p>
        <p class="byline">By <strong>{siteConfig.authorName}</strong> · Updated {new Date().toLocaleDateString("en-GB", { month: "long", year: "numeric" })}</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Boilie choice is where UK carp fishing gets tribal fast. Some anglers swear by one brand for a decade; others chop and change every
        session. The honest answer is that most premium boilies from the top UK brands out-perform each other by margins invisible on the
        bank, and consistency of application matters far more than which brand you back. But there are real category differences worth
        knowing.
      </p>

      <div class="aff-notice">
        <strong>Affiliate disclosure:</strong> The Amazon UK links below earn us a commission at no extra cost to you. It does not affect
        our picks. <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Details</a>.
      </div>

      <h2>Shelf-life vs freezer bait</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Shelf-life boilies</strong> use preservatives and are boiled harder to extend storage life at room temperature. Practical
        for casual anglers, less demanding logistically, and consistent in quality bag-to-bag. Top UK shelf-life examples: Mainline Cell,
        CC Moore Live System, Nash Scopex Squid.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Freezer baits</strong> use higher protein levels, stronger attractors, and no preservatives - they must be frozen until use.
        Generally out-fish shelf-life on pressured waters where fish are educated. Top UK freezer bait examples: Sticky Baits The Krill,
        Nash Instant Action, Mainline Response Range.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Which to choose:</strong> if you fish more than 15 sessions a year on the same water, freezer bait pays back. If you fish
        occasionally or across multiple venues, shelf-life is more practical and the performance gap is marginal.
      </p>

      <h2>Fishmeal vs birdfood vs milk protein</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Fishmeal-based boilies</strong> are the UK all-year default. Salt profile that matches carp's natural diet, works cold water
        and warm, forgiving of feeding mistakes. Cell, Krill, Live System are all fishmeal-heavy.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Birdfood/nut boilies</strong> (Nash Scopex, Fox Complex T, some CC Moore ranges) shine in warmer water - May through
        September on most UK lakes. Higher sugar content, faster metabolism response from active fish. Under-performs in water below 10°C.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Milk-protein baits</strong> are still specialist. High casein content, distinctive attractor profile, favoured on some
        specimen waters for big-fish selectivity. Rarely a first choice for newcomers.
      </p>

      <h2>Boilie size, and why it matters</h2>
      <p>The size number on the bag matters more than most anglers think:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>12 mm.</strong> Doubles as a hookbait size on stiff rigs; good for tench and bream mixed waters where you want to avoid smaller fish nuisance.</li>
        <li><strong>15-16 mm.</strong> UK default. Suits most 3-lb TC rods with standard hair rigs. Buy this if unsure.</li>
        <li><strong>18-20 mm.</strong> Sorts through smaller fish faster on waters with lots of low-double stock; also easier to spot on a rig against dark lake beds.</li>
        <li><strong>24 mm+.</strong> Specimen bait sizes. Only makes sense on waters where the average fish is 25 lb+.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Pop-ups, wafters and hookbaits</h2>
      <p>The hookbait is often the most important part of a rig, and matching it to your freebies is a real skill.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bright pop-ups</strong> (yellow, pink, white) over a natural freebie bed are visually distinctive - carp home in on the odd-looking bait. Classic on new waters.</li>
        <li><strong>Matched-colour balanced hookbaits</strong> disappear into the bed of freebies and work better on pressured venues where fish are wary of standing-out baits.</li>
        <li><strong>Wafters</strong> (near-neutral buoyancy) sit slightly off the lake bed like a natural boilie that has just settled. Increasingly popular for spooky, pressured fish.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Two picks worth putting on the bank</h2>
      {baitPicks.map((p, i) => (
        <div class="product-card">
          <div class="row g-3 align-items-start">
            <div class="col">
              <div class="rank">{i === 0 ? "All-year default" : "Freezer bait pick"}</div>
              <h3><a href={`/reviews/${p.slug}/`}>{p.name}</a></h3>
              <p class="verdict">{p.verdict}</p>
              <div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-2">
                <a class="btn btn-amazon" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank" href={amazonProductLink({ asin: p.asin, brand: p.brand, product: p.name })}>See on Amazon UK</a>
                <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary" href={`/reviews/${p.slug}/`}>Full review</a>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-auto text-end">
              <div class="price">{money(p.price)}</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}

      <h2>How much bait per session</h2>
      <p>Baiting strategy matters more than bait choice for most UK anglers. Rough guidelines:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Short session (12-24 hours):</strong> 500 g-1 kg per rod as a starting bed, top up small handfuls after every fish.</li>
        <li><strong>48-hour session:</strong> 1-2 kg per rod as starting bed, 500 g refresh each morning.</li>
        <li><strong>Pre-baiting between sessions:</strong> 1-2 kg per swim, 2-3 times per week, if the water and rules allow. Transforms results on syndicate waters.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>The one-line summary.</strong> Pick one quality brand (Mainline, Sticky, CC Moore, Nash, Fox), stick with it for six
        sessions minimum, feed consistently, and change your rig before you change your bait. That is how anglers actually catch on
        pressured UK water.
      </div>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <details class="faq"><summary>{f.q}</summary><p>{f.a}</p></details>
      ))}

      <h2>Continue reading</h2>
      <div class="cat-grid">
        <a class="cat-card" href="/gear/boilies-bait/"><h3>All boilie reviews</h3><p>Every brand on our UK bait desk.</p><div class="meta"><span>Category</span><span>Read →</span></div></a>
        <a class="cat-card" href="/guides/uk-carp-session-costs/"><h3>UK carp season costs</h3><p>Why bait is the biggest annual spend.</p><div class="meta"><span>Guide</span><span>Read →</span></div></a>
        <a class="cat-card" href="/"><h3>The pillar gear guide</h3><p>Seven kit families and how they fit together.</p><div class="meta"><span>Pillar</span><span>Read →</span></div></a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
